The attached image, corresponding to Fig. 2A and 2B from the study, characterizes Nano-11 nanoparticles. The transmission electron micrograph (left panel) displays spherical particles with an irregular, "cauliflower-like" surface. The dynamic light scattering data (right panel) indicates a relatively uniform size distribution, with a Z-average hydrodynamic diameter ranging from 75 to 80 nm. These dendrimer-like α-D-glucan nanoparticles are derived from phytoglycogen, possessing a highly branched structure and high molecular density, which are key properties for their function as vaccine adjuvants.